{white}The computer terminal has evolved from humble beginnings in the{/} {yellow,bold}1960s{/}{white}.{/}
{white}Originally, terminals were physical devices -- teletypewriters that{/}
{white}allowed users to interact with mainframe computers through typed commands.{/}

{white}The first video terminals appeared in the{/} {yellow,bold}1970s{/}{white} replacing paper printouts{/}
{white}with cathode-ray tube displays. Then, with the rise of personal computers{/}
{white}in the{/} {yellow,bold}1980s{/}{white}, terminal emulators brought command-line interfaces to{/}
{white}graphical environments.{/} {brightGreen,bold}Unix{/} {white}systems{/} popularised shells like {brightCyan,bold}sh{/}{white},{/}{brightCyan,bold} csh{/}{white}, and{/}
{white}later {/}{brightCyan,bold}bash{/}{white}, making terminals powerful tools for system administration and{/}{white}
{/}{white}development.{/}{white}

{/}{white}Today's terminals blend their classic functionality with modern features{/}{white}
{/}{white}-- syntax highlighting, tabs, themes, and web-based implementations like{/}{white}
{/}{white}this one. Despite decades of GUI evolution, the terminal remains an{/}{white}
{/}{white}essential tool for developers, system admins, and power users worldwide.

{/}{white}The command line endures because text is universal, scriptable, and{/}{white}
{/}{white}efficient, proving that sometimes the oldest interfaces are the most{/}{white} 
{/}{brightGreen,bold}timeless{/}{white}.{/}{white}

